---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-11-21",
    modifiedTime: "2026-11-23",
    author: "Shaheer Ali Khan",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: [
      "horizon treadmill troubleshooting",
      "horizon treadmill not working",
      "horizon treadmill wont start",
      "horizon treadmill belt slipping",
      "horizon treadmill lube belt message",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Find Your Symptom", link: "#short-answer" }}
>
  <article class="ed-article" data-template="editorial">
    <div class="ed-col">
      <nav class="ed-crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/problems/">Fault Diagnosis</a>
        <span aria-hidden="true">›</span>
        <span>Troubleshooting</span>
      </nav>
      <h1 class="ed-title">Horizon Treadmill Troubleshooting: Won’t Start, Belt and Console Problems</h1>
      <p class="ed-dek">
        Most Horizon faults come down to the safety key, a power supply Horizon does not allow (a GFCI outlet, a power strip or a shared circuit), a running belt that needs a quarter-turn of tension or centring, or a LUBE message that stops the belt until it is cleared. Here is the order to check them in, using Horizon’s own help-centre procedures, and what to have ready when you call Horizon support.
      </p>

      <!-- Byline -->
      <div class="ed-byline">
        <img src="/authors/shaheer-ali-khan.png" alt="Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD" width="44" height="44" />
        <div class="ed-byline-text">
          By <a href="/editorial-standards/#who" rel="author">Shaheer Ali Khan, PhD</a><br />
          Updated November 23, 2026 · Exercise Physiology & Biomechanics Specialist · ACSM-EP, CSCS<br />
          <span class="ed-method">Based on manufacturer manuals and service guidance, and the faults owners report most.</span>
        </div>
        <a href="#short-answer" class="btn-ghost">Symptom Table</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ed-disclosure">
        Nothing on this page is a paid placement, and it carries no affiliate links. <a href="/editorial-standards/">How we review.</a>
      </p>

      <div class="ed-body prose prose-lg">
        <p class="ed-lead">
          Horizon Fitness is part of Johnson Health Tech, the group that also makes Matrix and Vision
          equipment, and its home treadmills — the T101, T202, T303 and the 7.0, 7.4 and 7.8 AT
          Studio Series — share a great deal of their troubleshooting. They also share a habit that
          catches owners out: Horizon is unusually strict about how the treadmill is powered, and a
          machine on the wrong outlet can look exactly like a broken one. Horizon publishes its
          fault-finding as short articles on its help centre rather than as a long list of numbered
          codes, and this page works through those articles in the order that finds the cause
          fastest, naming which Horizon document each step comes from.
        </p>

        <nav class="ed-toc not-prose" aria-label="Jump to">
          <h2>Jump to</h2>
          <ol>
            <li><a href="#short-answer">The Short Answer</a></li>
            <li><a href="#wont-start">Horizon Treadmill Won’t Turn On or Start</a></li>
            <li><a href="#safety-key">Safety Key, Dashes and a Display of 8s</a></li>
            <li><a href="#reset-update">Resetting the Console and Updating the Software</a></li>
            <li><a href="#belt-wont-move">Console Lit but the Belt Will Not Move, or Stops and Starts</a></li>
            <li><a href="#belt-slipping">Belt Slipping, Tension and Centring on a Horizon</a></li>
            <li><a href="#lubrication">Lubrication and the LUBE BELT Message</a></li>
            <li><a href="#messages">Horizon Error Messages and Codes</a></li>
            <li><a href="#bluetooth-apps">Bluetooth, Apps and Watch Connection Problems</a></li>
            <li><a href="#warranty-support">Warranty and Support</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
          </ol>
        </nav>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>When a Horizon treadmill will not start, check the safety key first, then the power
            supply: Horizon wants a grounded wall outlet on a dedicated 15-amp circuit, with no GFCI
            outlet, power strip, surge protector or extension cord.</strong> After that, most remaining
            faults are a belt that needs tension or centring, a deck that needs silicone, or a LUBE
            message that has to be cleared before the belt will move.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Dashes, 8s or SAFETY KEY OFF on the display</strong> means the key is not
            registering. Reseat it, and check a magnetic key is still magnetised.</li>
            <li><strong>A completely dark console</strong> is the outlet, the house breaker, the cord or
            the ON/OFF switch next to the power cord, in that order.</li>
            <li><strong>A belt that slips</strong> gets both rear roller bolts turned clockwise ¼ turn at a
            time, never more, until the slip goes.</li>
            <li><strong>A belt that drifts</strong> is centred at 1 mph, a ¼ turn at a time on the rear
            bolts, as described below.</li>
            <li><strong>A silicone deck</strong> needs lubricating every 3 months or 150 miles; a waxed deck
            needs nothing at all. Your manual says which you have.</li>
          </ul>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Horizon symptom</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Most likely cause</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">First fix</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Completely dead, no lights</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Outlet, tripped breaker, cord, ON/OFF switch</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Test the outlet with a lamp; check the switch beside the cord is on</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Display shows dashes, 8s or SAFETY KEY OFF</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Safety key not in place or not magnetised</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Reseat the key; replace it if the magnet is weak</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Shows LUBE or LUBE BELT, belt will not run</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lubrication reminder</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lubricate (silicone decks only), then clear it with your model’s key combination</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Console lit, belt will not move or moves briefly</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Power supply, drive belt, loose cable</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Move to a dedicated circuit; then call Horizon before opening the motor cover</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stops and resets, or speed is erratic</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Shared circuit, console cable, phone interference</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Plug straight into the wall; take the phone off the reading rack</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt slips or hesitates underfoot</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Stretched belt, dry silicone deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tension ¼ turn at a time; lubricate if due</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt drifts to one side</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Tracking, unlevel floor</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Level the frame; centre at 1 mph with the rear bolts</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Breaker trips mid-workout</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Shared circuit, GFCI, extension cord</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dedicated 15-amp circuit, plugged straight into the wall</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">App or watch will not connect</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Heart-rate strap mode, another app holding it</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hold the Bluetooth button 5 seconds; close other apps</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The start-up sequence itself — switch, key, START — is set out step by step in our guide on <a href="/how-to-start-a-horizon-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">how to start a Horizon treadmill</a>,
            and it is worth reading first if the machine is new to you. This page picks up where that
            one stops, with the faults. Owners of other brands will find the same structure in our <a href="/proform-treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">ProForm treadmill troubleshooting</a> and <a href="/nordictrack-treadmill-troubleshooting/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">NordicTrack treadmill troubleshooting</a> guides, but note that Horizon’s rules differ on one important point: ProForm and NordicTrack
            ask for a surge suppressor, while Horizon says not to use one.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="wont-start" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Horizon Treadmill Won’t Turn On or Start</h2>
<p>
            Horizon’s help centre splits a machine that will not start into two problems, and the fix
            depends on which one you have: a console that does not light at all, and a console that
            lights but a treadmill that will not run. Work out which before you do anything else.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The console does not light up</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon’s article “Treadmill Console Does Not Light Up” (checked November 2026) starts with a
            single question: does the red light on the ON/OFF switch come on? The switch on current
            models sits at the front of the frame next to the power cord, low down, not on the console;
            Horizon notes that some treadmills have no lit switch, and some older ones have the switch
            behind the console instead. If the switch light is off, Horizon’s checks are:
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>The outlet works.</strong> Plug a lamp into it.</li>
            <li><strong>The house breaker has not tripped.</strong> Reset it if it has.</li>
            <li><strong>The circuit is right.</strong> A dedicated 15-amp circuit, not a GFCI-equipped
            outlet, and not a power strip, <a href="/treadmill-surge-protector/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>surge protector</a> or extension cord.</li>
            <li><strong>The cord is sound</strong>, not pinched or damaged, and fully plugged in at the
            wall <em>and</em> at the machine.</li>
            <li><strong>The power switch is on.</strong></li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            If the switch light is on but the console stays dark, Horizon’s next step is to switch off,
            unplug, wait 60 seconds, remove the motor cover, wait for the red LEDs on the motor control
            board to go out, and then check that the console cable and the other wires on the lower board
            are seated and undamaged. That is work under the motor cover on an electrical appliance. If
            you are not comfortable with it, or the treadmill is in warranty, stop at this point and call
            Horizon; the support section at the end of this page lists what to have ready.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Why Horizon is strict about the outlet</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon’s “Treadmill Electrical Requirements” article (checked November 2026) says most of its
            North American treadmills run on 120 volts at 60 Hz and should be on a dedicated 15 to 20 amp
            circuit, meaning nothing else draws power from that circuit. It says GFCI outlets and surge
            protectors can cut power and are not compatible, that the treadmill should go straight into
            a grounded three-prong outlet, and that extension cords can overload or under-supply it.
            It also warns that ignoring this may void the warranty, and Horizon’s list of what the
            warranty does not cover includes damage from outlets or cords that do not meet its
            electrical requirements. The quickest test for a dedicated circuit is to switch that breaker
            off at the panel and see whether anything else in the house loses power. If you are unsure,
            ask an electrician; never fit a bigger breaker to stop trips.
          </p>
          <p>
            The same supply rules are Horizon’s first answer to a breaker that trips mid-workout and to a
            belt that stops and starts, so one visit from an electrician can clear several symptoms at
            once. Our general guide to a <a href="/treadmill-wont-turn-on/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill that won’t turn on</a> works through the same chain for any brand, from the outlet to the console cable.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety-key" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Safety Key, Dashes and a Display of 8s</h2>
<p>
            Horizon’s help centre is blunt about it: “Your treadmill will not start unless the safety key
            is placed in position.” Its article on a display showing only dashes, 8s or “Safety Key Off”
            (checked November 2026) adds that “the dashes or screen saver mode are an indicator of the
            safety key being off.” A Horizon showing dashes is therefore usually not broken or asleep in
            any deeper sense; it is waiting for the key.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Is it in place?</strong> Horizon describes the key as “either inserted or
            magnetically attached”, depending on the model. Take it off and refit it firmly.</li>
            <li><strong>Is a magnetic key still magnetised?</strong> Horizon lists this as a separate
            check. A key that only works when held, or that drops the treadmill out when the cord is
            brushed, is not holding the sensor.</li>
            <li><strong>Is it the right key?</strong> A key from another treadmill, or a loose magnet, may
            sit in the right place without triggering the sensor. Horizon sells replacement keys through
            its parts site, parts.horizonfitness.com.</li>
            <li><strong>Has it been tested lately?</strong> Horizon asks owners to check that the key stops
            the treadmill every two weeks, and to remove it whenever the machine is not in use.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Do not bypass the key. Horizon’s deeper troubleshooting for this symptom tests the <a href="/treadmill-speed-sensor/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>reed switch</a> the key acts on, which is service work rather than an owner fix. A treadmill that runs without
            a working key has lost the one control that stops the belt if you fall.
          </p>
          <p>
            If the key is in, the display still shows dashes and the key is known to be good, the reed
            switch or its wiring is the suspect, and that is a support call. Horizon’s help centre keeps a
            separate version of this article for the 7.0AT-03 and 7.0AT-04, so owners of those models
            should check the model-specific note before assuming a fault.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="reset-update" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Resetting the Console and Updating the Software</h2>
<p>
            “Reset” means three different things on a Horizon, and they do very different amounts of
            work.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The console reset</h3>
          <p>
            On the current T101, T202, 7.0 AT and 7.4 AT owner’s manuals, holding STOP for 3 seconds resets
            the console, clearing a program or menu that seems stuck. Try it before reaching for the <a href="/how-to-turn-on-a-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>power
            switch</a>.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">The power cycle</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon’s maintenance article (checked November 2026) asks owners to unplug the treadmill and
            wait two to three minutes before any maintenance, to let power drain from the electronics, and
            its wiring checks use a 60-second wait for the lower board to discharge. The same habit makes a
            better reset than a quick flick of the switch: key out, switch off, unplug at the wall, wait a
            few minutes, then plug in, switch on and fit the key.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">A software update</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon’s “Software and Firmware Updates” page (checked November 2026) recommends updating the
            console software before first use, and lists a file for each model version, from the T101-05
            and T101-08 to the T202-06 and the 7.0AT-05. Updates are loaded from a USB stick formatted to
            FAT32, following the PDF instructions for your console. Two cautions from that page matter:
            the updates are for North American machines only, and an update “will erase previously saved
            user data and reset your equipment’s console to its factory default settings.” Pick the file by
            the model version on your serial label (T101-07, T101-08 and so on), not by the name on the
            console, and note any saved users first.
          </p>
          <p>
            Software matters more on a Horizon than many owners realise. Clearing the LUBE message on a
            7.4AT-04 uses a different key combination once the console is on version 4.006 or newer, the
            stuck-key error codes only appear on consoles at a minimum software version, and Horizon’s
            fix for interference-related speed problems on some T101s is a software update. Horizon also
            publishes a master reset document for owners who cannot clear a LUBE message after updating;
            follow that document for your model rather than a sequence found for another machine.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt-wont-move" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Console Lit but the Belt Will Not Move, or Stops and Starts</h2>
<p>
            Horizon’s article “Treadmill <a href="/treadmill-turns-on-but-belt-wont-move/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Belt Does Not Move</a> or Moves Briefly” (checked November 2026)
            starts, again, with power: the treadmill should be on a dedicated 15-amp circuit, not a GFCI
            outlet, and not on a power strip, surge protector or extension cord. Only then does it move on
            to the machine.
          </p>
          <ol>
            <li><strong>Look for any movement.</strong> If the belt moves a little and stops, note how far
            or for how long; that detail helps support.</li>
            <li><strong>Try to move the belt by hand</strong>, with the machine unplugged. If it will not
            move, Horizon says the drive belt may have come off or the motor bearings may have seized.</li>
            <li><strong>Check the drive belt and connections.</strong> Horizon’s next step is to unplug,
            remove the plastic motor cover at the front, check the drive belt’s alignment and look for
            loose, crimped, damaged or burnt wires, which can work loose in shipping or assembly.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Before any of that, check the display. A LUBE or LUBE BELT message stops the belt by design,
            and is covered in the lubrication section below.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Stops and resets, or speed is erratic</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon’s “How to Fix a Treadmill that is Stopping and Starting” article (checked November 2026)
            lists the causes in this order: the power source (straight into the wall, on a 15-amp circuit
            it does not share with appliances, computers or TVs — Horizon says sharing can cause electronic
            components to fail), the console cable at the console board and at the joint between console
            and mast, the belt tension and lubrication (rollers and deck that feel hot after a short use
            can indicate lack of lubrication), and finally a recalibration to factory settings.
          </p>
          <p>
            A separate article on electromagnetic interference describes a flickering console with a belt
            that slows and speeds up, a console that switches off, numbers that read wrong, speed or
            incline that changes without input, and a repeating clicking from the motor control board.
            Horizon’s first fix is to take any phone or tablet off the reading rack, or put it in airplane
            mode, and see whether the symptoms stop. For T101s it names specific software versions to move
            to.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Check an older T101 against the recall</h3>
          <p>
            Speed that changes or stops without input on a T101-05 is a recall matter, not a maintenance
            one. In October 2022 the CPSC and Johnson Health Tech recalled T101-05 treadmills with serial
            numbers beginning TM734 or TM486 because they could accelerate, change speed or stop
            unexpectedly; the remedy was a free software repair. Our <a href="/horizon-t101-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon T101 review</a> sets out how to check your label and what the repair involves. If you cannot confirm the
            software was updated, stop using the machine and contact Horizon.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="belt-slipping" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Belt Slipping, Tension and Centring on a Horizon</h2>
<p>
            A belt that hesitates when your foot lands, or that creeps to one side, is the most common
            Horizon maintenance job, and Horizon expects it. Its “Running Belt Adjustments” article (checked
            November 2026) says temperature and humidity make the belt stretch, recommends checking tension
            and alignment after the first week of use, and then monthly as part of routine maintenance.
            Both jobs use the two hex bolts in the end caps at the rear of the deck, with the Allen wrench
            or T-handle wrench supplied with the treadmill.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Tensioning a slipping belt</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon’s “Tensioning and Centering the Running Belt” article (checked November 2026) gives the
            procedure: turn the treadmill off, then turn <strong>both</strong> rear roller bolts ¼ turn
            clockwise. Turn it back on and check for slipping. Repeat if needed, but “never turn the roller
            bolts more than ¼ turn at a time.” The belt is tensioned correctly “when the slipping sensation
            is gone.” The same article warns that the belt should not be moving while you tension it, and
            that over-tightening causes excessive wear on the treadmill and its components.
          </p>
          <p>
            To test for slip, Horizon’s adjustment article suggests walking at 3 mph on a level treadmill
            and, once you are balanced, stomping one foot as a heavy-footed runner would. If the belt stops
            or hesitates, add tension; if it does not, leave it alone. Turn each bolt the same amount so you
            do not knock the belt off centre while tensioning it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Centring a belt that drifts</h3>
          <p>
            Level the treadmill first; an unlevel frame is one of the causes Horizon lists for a belt that
            will not stay centred. Then, per the same article, run the belt at 1 mph — no faster while
            centring — and keep fingers, hair and clothing well away from it.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Problem</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Which bolt</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Turn</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Condition</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt slips</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Both rear roller bolts, equally</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">¼ turn clockwise, then test</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Treadmill off, belt not moving</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt too far to the right</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Right bolt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">¼ turn clockwise, then wait</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt running at 1 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt too far to the left</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Left bolt</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">¼ turn clockwise, then wait</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt running at 1 mph</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Belt too loose and off-centre</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Centre first, then tension</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">As above</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Remove the end caps for easier access</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            After each quarter turn, wait for the belt to settle before turning again; it moves more than
            you expect. Horizon suggests using the marking or part line on the motor cover as the reference
            for centre. Horizon’s help centre also carries a shorter article that centres the belt using
            only the left bolt — counter-clockwise when the belt has run to the right, clockwise when it has
            run to the left — so if your
            owner’s manual shows a different method, follow the manual for your model.
          </p>
          <p>
            A belt that still slips with correct tension and a lubricated deck is a worn belt, a glazed
            drive belt or a <a href="/treadmill-deck-replacement/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>worn deck</a>. Horizon’s advice is to inspect the belt for curling, fraying, rips and
            seam problems each time you lubricate. Our guide to a <a href="/treadmill-belt-slipping/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill belt that slips</a> explains why tightening is so often the wrong first move and when a belt is past adjusting.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="lubrication" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Lubrication and the LUBE BELT Message</h2>
<p>
            Lubrication is where Horizon owners most often get conflicting advice, because Horizon makes
            two kinds of running surface. Its article on deck lubrication (checked November 2026) says to
            check the owner’s manual to see whether your treadmill has a <strong>silicone</strong> or a <strong>waxed</strong> deck and belt. Waxed surfaces “are considered maintenance free”, and
            nothing should ever be applied to them. Silicone surfaces need periodic silicone, and running
            them dry causes friction that Horizon says can lead to premature failure of the belt, deck,
            motor or electronics.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How often, and with what</h3>
          <p>
            For silicone decks, Horizon recommends lubricating every 150 miles (241 km) or every 3 months,
            adjusted for use, age and conditions. For treadmills made after November 2002 it specifies 100%
            silicone oil, sold on its accessories page; for older machines it asks owners to call. The
            treadmill ships with a 40 ml bottle, enough for two applications. Do not substitute a
            household spray or a petroleum lubricant.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Horizon’s method</h3>
          <ol>
            <li>Power down and unplug the treadmill.</li>
            <li>Loosen the rear roller bolts enough to lift the belt and expose the centre of the deck.
            Horizon’s lubrication article puts this at 10 to 15 turns counter-clockwise.</li>
            <li>Lift the belt and apply the silicone in a zig-zag across the deck surface — 20 ml per
            application, never more than 30 ml at once — on the deck, not on top of the belt.</li>
            <li>Tighten both bolts clockwise by the same number of turns you loosened them.</li>
            <li>Plug in, fit the key, run the belt at 3 mph for 3 minutes with nobody on it, then walk on it
            for 3 minutes to spread the silicone and check tension and centring.</li>
            <li>Wipe any excess from the sides with a damp cloth.</li>
          </ol>
          <p>
            Horizon also describes a method with its silicone applicator that does not need the belt
            loosened: 10 ml slid under the belt from the side towards the rear roller, then repeated on the
            other half of the belt. Either way, re-check tension and alignment afterwards.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Clearing the LUBE message</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon’s treadmills display “LUBE” or “LUBE BELT” as a mileage reminder, and “the treadmill
            will not operate while the message is showing.” One Horizon article puts the reminder at 300
            miles while recommending lubrication every 150, and older documents vary too, so treat the
            message as a backstop rather than a schedule. Horizon’s reset article (checked November 2026)
            gives the combination by model:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Model</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Hold together</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">For</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T101, T202, T303</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed up (+) and STOP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 to 7 seconds</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.0 AT and older 7.4 AT</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed up (by the middle dial) and STOP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 to 7 seconds</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.4AT-04 on current software</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed key 12 and STOP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 seconds</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.4AT-04 on version 4.006 or newer</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Media Play/Pause and STOP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 seconds</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.8 AT</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Play/Pause and STOP</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 to 7 seconds</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Horizon notes that pressing any key suspends the message until after your next workout, and
            that some older models need STOP held for 5 seconds to suspend it for 5 miles. Suspending it is
            not the same as lubricating: on a silicone deck, do the job, then clear the message. If the
            message will not clear, update the console software and then use Horizon’s master reset
            document for your model. Folding models with gas shocks under the deck have a separate
            instruction: a Teflon-based spray on the shock’s silver tube, not silicone.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="messages" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Horizon Error Messages and Codes</h2>
<p>
            Horizon’s manuals and help centre are organised by symptom rather than by a long table of
            numbered codes, and we do not publish code meanings that Horizon itself does not. These are the
            messages Horizon documents on its help centre (checked November 2026), and what it says about
            each.
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">On the display</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What Horizon says</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What to do</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Dashes, 8s or SAFETY KEY OFF</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">The safety key is off</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Reseat the key; check a magnetic key is magnetised</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">LUBE or LUBE BELT</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lubrication reminder; the belt will not run while it shows</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lubricate a silicone deck, then clear it for your model</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed Range Error – Ending Workout</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">A safety cut-out when load and a high incline push the speed out of range</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lower the incline</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">An error code as soon as it is switched on</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Can mean a stuck key on the console overlay</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Use Horizon’s Stuck Key Error Codes guide to find which overlay</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">E1</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Linked to no belt movement</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Follow Horizon’s attached E1 document; check power first</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            The stuck-key codes only appear on consoles at or above a set software version, which Horizon
            lists as V5.004 for the 7.0AT-04, V4.006 for the 7.4AT-04, V3.002 for the 7.8AT-04 and S4.09 for
            the T101-07. On an older version, a stuck key may simply make a button misbehave.
          </p>
          <p>
            For any code not in that table, do not act on a code list compiled for another brand; codes do
            not carry across manufacturers or even across console generations. Write down the exact
            message, when it appears (at power-on, on pressing START, a few seconds after the belt starts,
            or after minutes of running) and what the belt and incline were doing, then look it up in your
            model’s manual or send it to Horizon support. A message that appears once and clears after a
            proper power cycle is often a momentary supply dip; a message that comes back every time is a
            fault.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bluetooth-apps" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Bluetooth, Apps and Watch Connection Problems</h2>
<p>
            Horizon consoles are button-and-dial consoles rather than touchscreens, and Horizon’s help centre
            says plainly that no subscription, Wi-Fi or download is needed to use the treadmill. Connection
            problems therefore never stop the belt; they only stop an app seeing it. Horizon’s articles
            (checked November 2026) cover three kinds of connection.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Apps such as Zwift and Peloton</h3>
          <p>
            Studio Series treadmills use the Bluetooth FTMS protocol to share speed and incline with apps.
            For Zwift, Horizon’s instructions are to switch the treadmill on, open Zwift’s pairing screen in
            the RUN section (not RIDE), tap the treadmill image and pick the Horizon from the list. Horizon
            publishes separate Peloton App+ pairing pages for the 7.0AT-04, 7.4AT-04 and 7.8AT-04, and
            notes that the T303 lacks FTMS and will not send speed to Zwift. If the app cannot find the
            treadmill, close every other app that might already be connected to it, including Horizon’s own
            app, and try again.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Heart-rate strap versus app mode</h3>
          <p>
            This is the one that catches most owners. Holding the treadmill’s Bluetooth button for
            5 seconds switches it to receive a heart-rate strap’s signal, and in that mode, Horizon says,
            the treadmill cannot push data out to apps. Hold the button for another 5 seconds to switch
            back. When using Zwift, Horizon recommends connecting both the treadmill and the strap directly
            to the app instead. A strap that will not read may also need its electrodes slightly dampened.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Console speakers</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon says the console speakers should pair automatically with your phone or tablet when the
            treadmill is switched on. If they do not, open Settings, then Bluetooth, on the device and
            select the speakers under My Devices. Owner’s manuals add that after the treadmill sleeps or is
            switched off, it looks for the last paired music device on the next power-up.
          </p>
          <p>
            Watch tap-to-connect for <a href="/best-treadmills-for-apple-watch/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Apple Watch</a> and Galaxy Watch is listed for the 7.0 AT, and its steps
            are in our start guide linked above. If you are still weighing up the range, our <a href="/horizon-fitness/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Horizon Fitness brand guide</a> sets out which models have which connectivity.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty-support" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Serial Number, Warranty and Calling Horizon Support</h2>
<p>
            Almost every next step — the right software file, the right manual, a part or a warranty
            claim — starts with the serial number and the model version.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Finding the serial number</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon’s help centre (checked November 2026) says the serial number is often on a white label,
            and that the first page of the assembly instructions shows where it is on your model. On
            treadmills, frame serial numbers are on the front of the treadmill on the elevation rack; current
            owner’s manuals place the white barcode label near the ON/OFF switch and power cord. Some models
            use a console shared with other products, which carries its own serial number on the back
            cover of the console. Write both down, along with the model version (such as T101-08 or
            7.4AT-04), which differs from the marketing name.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">What the warranty covers</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon’s standard home warranties, as listed on its help centre in November 2026:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Treadmill</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Frame</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Motor</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Parts</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Labor</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T101, T202</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">T303, 7.0 AT</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">3 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">1 year</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">7.4 AT, 7.8 AT</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Lifetime</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">5 years</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">2 years</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Horizon asks owners to register within 30 days of purchase at horizonfitness.com/support/register
            and to keep proof of purchase, which it requires for all warranty claims. Its list of
            exclusions is worth reading before you troubleshoot: normal wear and tear, improper assembly or
            maintenance, use in a non-temperature-controlled space such as a garage or porch, a user over
            the maximum weight, outlets or cords that do not meet its electrical requirements, commercial
            use, equipment outside the US and Canada, and damage from repairs or attempted repairs. Horizon
            says any repair attempt is at your own risk, so on a machine in warranty, call before you take
            the motor cover off. Out of warranty, parts are sold at parts.horizonfitness.com. Whether that
            cover is good value against rivals is part of our verdict on <a href="/is-horizon-a-good-treadmill-brand/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">whether Horizon is a good treadmill brand</a>.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Who to call, and what to have ready</h3>
          <p>
            Horizon’s contact page (checked November 2026) lists in-warranty parts and tech support on
            800.244.4192, Monday to Friday 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. Central, or techsupport@horizonfitness.com. Out of
            warranty, and for general customer support, the number is 888.878.9011. Horizon asks you to have
            ready:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>The model number and serial number.</li>
            <li>The nature of the problem: error messages, symptoms, what is not working, and pictures.</li>
            <li>Your name, address and phone number.</li>
            <li>Proof of purchase, for any warranty claim.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Horizon also suggests being near the treadmill when you call, in case there are simple checks
            to try before it sends a technician. A short phone video of the fault, and a note of what you
            have already tried from this page, will save time on the call.
          </p>
        </div>


        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Mistakes to Avoid With Horizon Treadmill Troubleshooting</h2>
          <p>Horizon faults are usually simple. These four are how owners make them take longer or cost more than they should.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Plugging it into a surge protector or GFCI outlet</h3>
              <p>Many brands ask for a surge suppressor; Horizon says the opposite. A Horizon on a power strip, surge protector, extension cord, GFCI outlet or shared circuit can stay dark, stop and start or trip the breaker, and Horizon excludes damage from the wrong supply from its warranty. Fix the circuit before you suspect the machine.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Lubricating a waxed deck</h3>
              <p>Horizon makes both silicone and waxed running surfaces, and the waxed ones are maintenance-free: nothing should ever be applied to them. Check your owner’s manual before reaching for the bottle. On a silicone deck, use 100% silicone, about 20 ml, applied to the deck rather than the top of the belt.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Cranking the belt bolts more than a quarter turn</h3>
              <p>Horizon’s rule is a quarter turn at a time on both rear bolts, testing in between, and stopping as soon as the slip is gone. Big turns over-tighten the belt, wear the rollers and motor, and usually knock it off centre so that you then chase it from side to side.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Opening the motor cover before calling Horizon</h3>
              <p>Several of Horizon’s own checks involve removing the motor cover, but its warranty terms say repair attempts are at your own risk. On a machine still in warranty, call 800.244.4192 first with the model, serial number, a photo or video of the fault and proof of purchase.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>The fix, in short</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">Key, outlet, then the belt.</strong> Most Horizon problems are a
            safety key that is not registering, a treadmill on a GFCI outlet, power strip or shared circuit,
            or a LUBE message that stops the belt until it is cleared. Horizon’s own help centre puts the
            power supply first in almost every fault, and so should you.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">For the rest, follow Horizon’s quarter-turn rule and your manual.</strong> Tension both rear bolts a quarter turn at a time, centre at 1 mph, lubricate only a silicone deck,
            and call 800.244.4192 with your serial number before opening the motor cover on a machine in
            warranty. Our <a href="/how-to-start-a-horizon-treadmill/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Horizon start guide</a> covers the start-up sequence model by model.
          </p>
        </div>

