        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Why won’t my Horizon treadmill start?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually the safety key: Horizon says the treadmill will not start unless the key is in position, and a display of dashes, 8s or SAFETY KEY OFF means it is not registering. If nothing lights, check the outlet, house breaker, cord and the ON/OFF switch next to the power cord, and make sure it is on a dedicated 15-amp circuit with no GFCI, power strip or extension cord.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I reset a Horizon treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Hold STOP for 3 seconds to reset the console on current T101, T202, 7.0 AT and 7.4 AT models. For a fuller reset, remove the key, switch off, unplug and wait a few minutes before powering back up. A software update resets the console to factory settings and erases saved users, so use it only when Horizon’s instructions call for it.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I clear the LUBE BELT message on a Horizon treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              After lubricating, hold Speed up and STOP together for 5 to 7 seconds on the T101, T202, T303, 7.0 AT and older 7.4 AT. The 7.8 AT uses Play/Pause and STOP, and the 7.4AT-04 uses Speed key 12 and STOP, or Media Play/Pause and STOP on version 4.006 or newer. The belt will not run while the message shows.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How do I tighten a slipping belt on a Horizon treadmill?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Turn the treadmill off and turn both rear roller bolts clockwise a quarter turn each with the supplied wrench, then test. Horizon says never to turn the bolts more than a quarter turn at a time and to stop once the slipping sensation is gone, because over-tightening wears the treadmill.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What lubricant does a Horizon treadmill use?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For treadmills made after November 2002, Horizon specifies 100% silicone oil, about 20 ml per application every 3 months or 150 miles. That applies only to silicone decks. Horizon says waxed decks and belts are maintenance-free and nothing should be applied to them, so check your owner’s manual first.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Can I plug a Horizon treadmill into a surge protector?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. Horizon says to plug the treadmill directly into a grounded outlet on a dedicated 15 to 20 amp circuit, with no surge protector, power strip, extension cord or GFCI outlet, and warns that not doing so may void the warranty. This differs from some other brands, so follow Horizon’s rule on a Horizon.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the Horizon treadmill support phone number?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Horizon lists 800.244.4192 for in-warranty parts and tech support, Monday to Friday 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. Central, and techsupport@horizonfitness.com. Out of warranty, call 888.878.9011. Have the model number, serial number, a description or photos of the fault and proof of purchase ready.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
            database. Where sources disagree we say so rather than pick a number, we say plainly when a
            machine has not been tested hands-on, and we never accept payment for a placement. <a href="/editorial-standards/">Read our review process.</a>
          </p>
